          "text": "Possibly — but I’d need to know which method and which claimed problem to judge it fairly.\n\nThat said, a lot of “methods” don’t truly solve the underlying problem; they often:\n\n- **shift the problem elsewhere**\n- **work only under ideal assumptions**\n- **reduce symptoms rather than fix causes**\n- **solve a simplified version of the problem**\n- **depend on hidden costs or constraints**\n- **fail on edge cases that matter in practice**\n\nSo if your instinct is “this doesn’t actually solve what it claims,” that may be right — especially if the method only demonstrates improvement in a narrow setting but doesn’t address the core failure mode.\n\nSend me the method or the claim, and I’ll give you a blunt assessment.",
